WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.283
Programming begins before code.

2
00:00:02.283 --> 00:00:08.425
Start by stating what information enters the
program, what result must leave it, and which

3
00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:10.063
constraints must always hold.

4
00:00:10.063 --> 00:00:16.530
For a grade calculator, inputs may be scores
and weights; the output may be a numeric

5
00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.742
average and category.

6
00:00:17.742 --> 00:00:22.257
Write ordinary examples, boundary examples,
and invalid examples.

7
00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:27.181
This creates a behavioral contract that can be
checked independently of syntax.

8
00:00:27.181 --> 00:00:31.045
Then identify the transformations between
input and output.

9
00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:36.413
A clear contract prevents a common failure:
writing statements that run without knowing

10
00:00:36.413 --> 00:00:38.890
whether they solve the intended problem.

11
00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:45.199
Code is an executable representation of a
model, so the model must be understandable

12
00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:45.650
first.

13
00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.908
A program stores information as values.

14
00:00:48.908 --> 00:00:54.672
Numbers support arithmetic, strings represent
text, Boolean values represent true-or-false

15
00:00:54.672 --> 00:00:58.130
conditions, and collections group related
values.

16
00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:03.793
A variable is a name bound to a value, not a
permanent box with one fixed meaning.

17
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:07.110
Assignment changes which value a name refers
to.

18
00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:13.362
Good names express the role of the data: total
cost is clearer than t. Representation choices

19
00:01:13.362 --> 00:01:15.707
affect the rest of the design.

20
00:01:15.707 --> 00:01:21.755
A date stored as one ambiguous string is
harder to validate than separate year, month,

21
00:01:21.755 --> 00:01:22.964
and day values.

22
00:01:22.964 --> 00:01:28.817
Ask what operations the program needs, what
invalid states are possible, and which

23
00:01:28.817 --> 00:01:31.969
representation makes correct behavior easiest
to express.

24
00:01:32.114 --> 00:01:34.206
Expressions compute values.

25
00:01:34.206 --> 00:01:39.796
Arithmetic operators combine numbers;
comparison operators produce Boolean results;

26
00:01:39.796 --> 00:01:42.280
Boolean operators combine conditions.

27
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.410
Evaluation order matters.

28
00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:49.744
Multiplication normally occurs before
addition, but parentheses make the intended

29
00:01:49.744 --> 00:01:52.944
grouping explicit and easier to review.

30
00:01:52.944 --> 00:01:57.944
Avoid packing an entire algorithm into one
clever expression.

31
00:01:57.944 --> 00:02:03.109
Intermediate names can expose meaning and
provide places to inspect behavior.

32
00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:09.152
Types matter too: adding numbers differs from
concatenating text, and converting user input

33
00:02:09.152 --> 00:02:10.546
should be deliberate.

34
00:02:10.546 --> 00:02:15.784
When an expression behaves unexpectedly,
evaluate it from the inside outward, inspect

35
00:02:15.784 --> 00:02:21.895
each intermediate value, and confirm that the
data type and unit match the intended

36
00:02:21.895 --> 00:02:22.331
calculation.

37
00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.825
A conditional represents a decision.

38
00:02:24.825 --> 00:02:29.391
Each condition should correspond to a
meaningful rule in the problem.

39
00:02:29.391 --> 00:02:31.840
Order matters when ranges overlap.

40
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.722
In a grading rule, check the highest threshold
first or express non-overlapping intervals

41
00:02:38.722 --> 00:02:39.251
explicitly.

42
00:02:39.251 --> 00:02:44.659
Include a final else only when a meaningful
default or invalid case exists.

43
00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:49.979
Deeply nested conditions are difficult to
reason about, so simplify repeated tests and

44
00:02:49.979 --> 00:02:54.480
extract named helper functions when a decision
has its own concept.

45
00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:01.172
Test conditions at boundaries: exactly zero,
exactly the threshold, just below, and just

46
00:03:01.172 --> 00:03:01.687
above.

47
00:03:01.687 --> 00:03:07.246
A condition is correct only when both the true
path and the false path match the

48
00:03:07.246 --> 00:03:07.593
specification.

49
00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.848
Loops express repetition.

50
00:03:09.848 --> 00:03:16.376
A for loop naturally processes each item in a
collection, while a while loop repeats until a

51
00:03:16.376 --> 00:03:17.144
condition changes.

52
00:03:17.144 --> 00:03:22.233
Every loop needs a clear initialization,
update, and termination argument.

53
00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:26.506
An invariant describes what remains true after
each iteration.

54
00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:32.862
In a running total, the invariant may be that
total equals the sum of all items processed so

55
00:03:32.862 --> 00:03:33.215
far.

56
00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:37.157
This idea makes loops easier to prove and
debug.

57
00:03:37.157 --> 00:03:42.628
Watch for off-by-one errors, empty
collections, and updates that never occur.

58
00:03:42.628 --> 00:03:48.567
Print tracing can help during development, but
a final design should make the loop's purpose

59
00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:52.922
visible through names and structure rather
than relying on debugging output.

60
00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:56.880
A function gives one piece of behavior a name
and a boundary.

61
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.442
Parameters describe what the function needs;
the return value describes what it produces.

62
00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:09.228
Prefer a function that completes one coherent
responsibility over a long function that reads

63
00:04:09.228 --> 00:04:15.044
input, calculates, prints, modifies global
state, and handles every error at once.

64
00:04:15.044 --> 00:04:21.322
Pure calculation functions are especially easy
to test because the same inputs produce the

65
00:04:21.322 --> 00:04:23.564
same outputs without hidden dependencies.

66
00:04:23.564 --> 00:04:29.100
Avoid using global variables when a parameter
or return value would make the dependency

67
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:29.495
explicit.

68
00:04:29.495 --> 00:04:34.632
Good decomposition follows concepts in the
problem: validate score, compute weighted

69
00:04:34.632 --> 00:04:40.235
average, and format result are meaningful
units that can be reviewed separately.

70
00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.342
Collections are not interchangeable
containers.

71
00:04:43.342 --> 00:04:47.322
A list preserves order and can contain
repeated values.

72
00:04:47.322 --> 00:04:51.659
A tuple represents an ordered grouping that is
not changed in place.

73
00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:54.899
A set emphasizes membership and uniqueness.

74
00:04:54.899 --> 00:04:58.916
A dictionary maps keys to values for direct
lookup.

75
00:04:58.916 --> 00:05:02.552
Choose according to the operations the problem
requires.

76
00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:08.582
If student records are repeatedly retrieved by
identifier, a dictionary may express the

77
00:05:08.582 --> 00:05:11.365
relationship better than scanning a list.

78
00:05:11.365 --> 00:05:15.205
If order matters, a set alone is insufficient.

79
00:05:15.205 --> 00:05:21.374
Consider empty collections, duplicate keys or
values, and whether mutation should be

80
00:05:21.374 --> 00:05:21.888
allowed.

81
00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:27.775
Representation is part of algorithm design
because it determines both clarity and

82
00:05:27.775 --> 00:05:28.266
performance.

83
00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:34.747
Programs interact with files, users, networks,
and data that may be missing or malformed.

84
00:05:34.747 --> 00:05:41.503
Validate predictable constraints close to the
boundary: required fields, numeric ranges, and

85
00:05:41.503 --> 00:05:42.629
allowed choices.

86
00:05:42.629 --> 00:05:47.777
Exceptions handle operations that fail despite
the intended call, such as converting

87
00:05:47.777 --> 00:05:50.780
nonnumeric text or opening a missing file.

88
00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.900
Catch only exceptions the program can respond
to meaningfully.

89
00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:00.652
A broad catch that silently continues can hide
defects and corrupt results.

90
00:06:00.652 --> 00:06:06.584
Error messages should explain what failed and
what the user can do next without exposing

91
00:06:06.584 --> 00:06:06.979
secrets.

92
00:06:06.979 --> 00:06:12.323
Separate the normal path from recovery so that
correct behavior remains readable.

93
00:06:12.323 --> 00:06:19.275
Robustness is not the absence of errors; it is
controlled, observable behavior when errors

94
00:06:19.275 --> 00:06:19.772
occur.

95
00:06:19.922 --> 00:06:24.451
Testing converts the behavioral contract into
repeatable evidence.

96
00:06:24.451 --> 00:06:28.711
Begin with a small normal case whose answer
can be calculated by hand.

97
00:06:28.711 --> 00:06:34.589
Add boundary cases, such as an empty
collection, one item, the maximum allowed

98
00:06:34.589 --> 00:06:37.754
value, and values immediately around a
threshold.

99
00:06:37.754 --> 00:06:41.185
Add invalid cases and confirm the intended
error.

100
00:06:41.185 --> 00:06:47.198
Each test should identify one behavior and
produce a clear failure when that behavior

101
00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:47.627
changes.

102
00:06:47.627 --> 00:06:53.718
Tests do not prove the absence of every
defect, but they protect known requirements

103
00:06:53.718 --> 00:06:55.458
and make refactoring safer.

104
00:06:55.458 --> 00:07:01.521
When a defect is found, first create a test
that reproduces it, then repair the code and

105
00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:04.374
keep the test as a permanent regression check.

106
00:07:04.514 --> 00:07:07.410
A dependable workflow is iterative.

107
00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:11.453
Define inputs, outputs, constraints, and
examples.

108
00:07:11.453 --> 00:07:15.242
Choose representations that support the
required operations.

109
00:07:15.242 --> 00:07:18.316
Write the smallest correct step and observe
it.

110
00:07:18.316 --> 00:07:21.708
Add conditions and loops with explicit
reasoning.

111
00:07:21.708 --> 00:07:25.599
Extract functions around coherent
responsibilities.

112
00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:29.859
Validate external input and handle recoverable
failures.

113
00:07:29.859 --> 00:07:33.507
Turn examples and boundaries into automated
tests.

114
00:07:33.507 --> 00:07:37.474
Then revise names and structure without
changing behavior.

115
00:07:37.474 --> 00:07:42.410
This process scales better than writing the
whole program and debugging at the end.

116
00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:48.022
Syntax makes instructions executable, but
reliability comes from the chain of reasoning

117
00:07:48.022 --> 00:07:54.570
that connects the original problem to data,
control flow, functions, tests, and a result

118
00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.441
another person can understand.

119
00:07:56.441 --> 00:08:01.832
Before calling the program complete, ask
another person to run one normal example and

120
00:08:01.832 --> 00:08:04.527
one failure case from the written contract.

121
00:08:04.527 --> 00:08:10.096
Their questions often reveal hidden
assumptions about units, input format, or

122
00:08:10.096 --> 00:08:11.109
expected output.

123
00:08:11.109 --> 00:08:17.273
Record those assumptions as validation rules,
documentation, or tests so the next revision

124
00:08:17.273 --> 00:08:19.170
begins with stronger evidence.
